{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"relevant-liberal-xantus","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nRP-04 ordinary configuration is approved, but its secrets/authentication design is intentionally unapproved. Rebar must safely support Bedrock, OpenAI, Anthropic, Gerrit, GitHub, GitLab, Jenkins, Jira Cloud, and Jira Data Center across local developer workstations, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, AWS-hosted services, CLI, library, MCP, and hosted-service consumers. A universal secret broker or hand-rolled credential store could weaken SDK-native refresh, workload identity, credential-helper protection, rotation, and redaction.\n\n## Approach\nRemain in architecture RESEARCH and use rebar-brainstorm. Inventory current Rebar credential boundaries and external dependencies. Consult current official documentation/source for each provider and environment, plus mature actively maintained OSS prior art. Produce a provider/environment matrix that identifies credential shape, authoritative mechanism, refresh/rotation owner, injection source, whether Rebar needs raw material, and failure/redaction obligations. Critically compare a generic secret-provider abstraction against typed capability/provider seams and direct delegation to official SDK/helper chains. Record constraints separately from Rebar-owned choices. No ADR, epic, implementation tickets, or production changes until the maintainer confirms the written findings and approves the design through dialogue.\n\n## Scope\nRead-only repository, tracker, dependency-source, and web research. Checkout-local ignored findings live at `.joe-janitor/rp04-secrets-research.md`; durable conclusions are commented here. Never read `~/.zshrc`, disclose credentials, invoke secrets through argv, or persist raw credentials.\n\n## Verification\nCross-check official mechanisms against current Rebar call sites and at least several mature OSS implementations. Every matrix claim cites a current primary source where available. Identify unsupported or ambiguous cases explicitly rather than inventing a credential protocol. Audit the written artifact for absence of secret values and for coverage of all named providers and environments.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The written dependency findings cover all named providers and environments, cite what was consulted, state actual credential/refresh behavior, and distinguish external constraints from Rebar-owned choices.\n- [ ] A provider/environment matrix records credential shape, preferred authoritative mechanism, refresh/rotation ownership, local/CI/AWS-hosted source, raw-secret exposure, and fail-safe behavior.\n- [ ] Mature actively maintained OSS prior art is compared, including how projects delegate to SDK chains, credential helpers, workload identity, CI credential binding, keyrings, or external secret managers.\n- [ ] The research critically evaluates universal string lookup, typed credential/provider capabilities, direct ambient delegation, subprocess handoff, library injection, MCP/CLI trust boundaries, redaction, and rotation.\n- [ ] `.joe-janitor/rp04-secrets-research.md` is read back, contains no credential values, and is summarized durably on this ticket.\n- [ ] The maintainer receives exactly one evidence-backed RP-04 secrets decision question; no ADR, epic, child stories, or production code are created in this phase.","id":"2704-39ae-16f5-4e96","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["janitor","recovery","research","security"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"RP-04 secrets and authentication dependency research"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786931951571979001,"uuid":"9f670582-2d0d-4fbb-8db5-b27107666c38"}